#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138166 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a35a2fee-a1a5-59aa-9390-f73d2898320c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Constant of Variation"}] :question/id #uuid "f01f91de-9010-5429-ba83-faaf90c866d7" :question/math-academy-id "e-14086" :question/problem "The force $F,$ measured in $\\text{kg}\\, \\text m / \\text s^2$, applied to a motorcycle varies directly with its acceleration $a,$ measured in $\\textrm{m}/\\textrm{s}^2.$ When the acceleration of the motorcycle is $a=5\\,\\textrm{m}/\\textrm{s}^2,$ the force is $F=2\\,000\\,\\text{kg}\\, \\text m / \\text s^2.$ What is the constant of variation?" :question/worked-solution "The force $F$ is directly proportional to the acceleration $a,$ so we have\n\n$$\nF = k{a},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when the acceleration is $a=5\\, \\textrm{m}/\\,\\textrm{s}^2,$ the force is $F=2\\,000\\,\\text{kg}\\, \\text m / \\text s^2.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\,000 & =k\\cdot (5) \\\\ 2\\,000 & =5k \\\\ \\frac{2\\,000}{5} & =k \\\\ 400 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k = \\dfrac{F}{a},$ we have that the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{kg m/s}}^{2}}{\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{kg}\\cancel{{\\text{m/s}}^{2}}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\text{kg}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is $k = 400\\,\\textrm{kg}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138168 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7df32abc-07bd-5213-a838-50e893ff5759" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling With Direct Variation"}] :question/id #uuid "f1408bad-ae5c-55fb-b78e-60ee0d8a1ca0" :question/math-academy-id "e-14088" :question/problem "The stretch $s,$ measured in centimeters $(\\textrm{cm}),$ of a loaded spring varies directly with the load $l,$ measured in grams $(\\mathrm{g}).$ When the load is $l=8\\,\\mathrm{g},$ the stretch is $s=9.6\\,\\mathrm{cm}.$ Find the load when the stretch is $s=7.2\\,\\mathrm{cm}.$" :question/worked-solution "The stretch $s$ is directly proportional to the load $l,$ so we have\n\n$$\ns = k{l},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when $l=8\\,\\mathrm{g},$ we have $s=9.6\\,\\mathrm{cm}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9.6 & =k\\cdot (8) \\\\ 9.6 & =8k \\\\ \\frac{9.6}{8} & =k \\\\ 1.2 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\ns = 1.2l.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the load when the stretch is $s=7.2\\,\\mathrm{cm},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l & =\\frac{s}{1.2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7.2}{1.2} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the load when $s=7.2\\,\\mathrm{cm}$ is $l=6 \\,\\textrm{g}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138171 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01db3ebe-66ec-5340-8e29-c99fb149f5da" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Direct Variations With Second Powers"}] :question/id #uuid "201cba9a-1d9e-5bf3-b312-d84fb3d59407" :question/math-academy-id "e-14091" :question/problem "The force $F$, in newtons ($\\mathrm N$), that an elastic spring exerts on an object varies directly with the **square** of the extension of the spring $L,$ measured in meters. If the force is $F = 4\\,\\mathrm N$ when $L=0.1\\,\\mathrm m,$ then what equation models this situation?" :question/worked-solution "The force $F$ is directly proportional to the square of the spring extension $L$, so we have\n\n$$\nF = kL^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $F=4\\,\\textrm{N}$ when $L=0.1\\,\\textrm m.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4 & =k\\cdot (0.1)^{2} \\\\ 4 & =k\\cdot 0.01 \\\\ \\frac{4}{0.01} & =k \\\\ 400 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our direct variation equation is\n\n$$\nF = 400L^2.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7df32abc-07bd-5213-a838-50e893ff5759" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling With Direct Variation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18a2f508-0d3f-5485-b4c9-6bc0c4278ca5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,\\text{gal}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498781} {:answer/id #uuid "8e1fd269-64ac-5cb9-a215-b63ceb27fc80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{gal}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499041} {:answer/id #uuid "ab1c1d7e-46a2-51a8-b2a2-114e1301182c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\text{gal}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499106} {:answer/id #uuid "dfbebcfc-0bf6-59d0-b330-74754c1eba0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27\\,\\text{gal}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499229} {:answer/id #uuid "eb3d929f-a284-5d04-9531-bbd2d160416a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.25\\,\\text{gal}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e1fd269-64ac-5cb9-a215-b63ceb27fc80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\,\\text{gal}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499041} :answer-field/id #uuid "66ac539c-1bae-55e9-b4d4-dd3955107d53" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499364}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "db05b6d0-7f2f-569f-a84a-34026d2fb3c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-116379" :question/problem "The amount of bleach $b,$ measured in gallons ($gal$), in a certain detergent product varies directly with the amount of water $w,$ measured in gallons, in the product. When the amount of bleach is $b=1.25\\,gal,$ the amount of water is $w=3.75\\,gal.$ Find the amount of bleach when the amount of water is $w=9\\,gal.$" :question/worked-solution "The amount of bleach $b$ is directly proportional to the amount of water $w,$ so we have\n\n$$\nb=kw.\n$$\n\nWe are told that when the amount of bleach is $b=1.25\\,gal,$ the amount of water is $w=3.75\\,gal.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.25 & =k\\cdot (3.75) \\\\ 1.25 & =3.75k \\\\ \\frac{1.25}{3.75} & =k \\\\ \\frac{1}{3} & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\nb=\\frac{1}{3}w.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the amount of bleach when the amount of water is  $w=9\\,gal,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nb=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (9)=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, the amount of bleach when $w=9\\,gal$ is $b=3\\,\\text{gal}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01db3ebe-66ec-5340-8e29-c99fb149f5da" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Direct Variations With Second Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c4b80d1-6933-5dca-8089-68049788a17c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C=\\frac{1}{20}d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498754} {:answer/id #uuid "21a503e1-50cb-5b32-98a2-2a6c31f0e6ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C=80{d}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498804} {:answer/id #uuid "952f0fad-eee9-5eee-ad0d-09f697bb097f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C=\\frac{1}{80}{d}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499053} {:answer/id #uuid "cc4c9be9-340f-5193-9dc3-ebe2c288db65" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C=\\frac{1}{6\\,400}{d}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499181} {:answer/id #uuid "e6312e0e-4657-594a-abdf-411d6e729836" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C=20d" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499252}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "952f0fad-eee9-5eee-ad0d-09f697bb097f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C=\\frac{1}{80}{d}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499053} :answer-field/id #uuid "270ef63f-3e5e-536c-9d39-f7c20c134b1a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bcc34549-4b02-5866-8337-252ca34764e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-116482" :question/problem "At a car rental agency, the charge $C,$ in dollars, for renting a car varies directly with the **square** of the distance  $d$ traveled by the car, measured in miles. If it costs $$5$ to rent a car to drive $20$ miles, then what equation models this situation?" :question/worked-solution "The charge $C$ is directly proportional to the square of the distance $d,$ so we have\n\n$$\nC=k{d}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $C=$5$ when $d=20\\,\\text{mi}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =k\\cdot {20}^{2} \\\\ 5 & =k\\cdot 400 \\\\ \\frac{5}{400} & =k \\\\ \\frac{1}{80} & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our direct variation equation is\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{80}{d}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499473 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138432 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01db3ebe-66ec-5340-8e29-c99fb149f5da" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Direct Variations With Second Powers"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12b979b7-6e4f-5676-8218-ccaffa35d740" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C=6.66v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498770} {:answer/id #uuid "6887182b-a952-50cd-8cb0-b8ad15fa5b51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C=667{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498967} {:answer/id #uuid "6fed4ddb-cbbf-5f1c-a210-5f9a64c7d92c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C=0.0015{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498979} {:answer/id #uuid "ca2276a8-b089-5eaf-91b4-9feb332e28c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C=0.15v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499173} {:answer/id #uuid "dd0a6a5d-4232-555b-8922-a61347f972aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C=0.0225{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499221}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fed4ddb-cbbf-5f1c-a210-5f9a64c7d92c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C=0.0015{v}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498979} :answer-field/id #uuid "a8b9ec7b-af39-5a0f-ac6b-c6ef3096bc15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "58ce1cea-1782-532b-bc46-409e39fb6f1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-116487" :question/problem "The fuel cost $C,$ in dollars per hour, for running a train is directly proportional to the **square** of its speed $v,$ measured in miles per hour. If it costs $$15$ per hour to run a train that moves at a speed of $100$ miles per hour, what equation models this situation?" :question/worked-solution "The fuel cost $C$ is directly proportional to the square of the speed $v,$ so we have\n\n$$\nC=k{v}^{2},\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that $C=15$ dollars per hour when $v=100\\,\\text{mph}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}15 & =k\\cdot {100}^{2} \\\\ 15 & =k\\cdot 10\\,000 \\\\ \\frac{15}{10\\,000} & =k \\\\ 0.0015 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our direct variation equation is\n\n$$\nC=0.0015{v}^{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a35a2fee-a1a5-59aa-9390-f73d2898320c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Constant of Variation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f94ed45-75ca-5605-8b5a-a84fd9be3ec0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$9$ per kilowatt-hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498799} {:answer/id #uuid "ca5eac2f-debe-569b-8366-35580cfe8d78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$12$ per kilowatt-hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499174} {:answer/id #uuid "ce533a25-089b-537e-8dde-61ffcb8f0059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$11$  per kilowatt-hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499185} {:answer/id #uuid "e7ca6b9e-1e40-5500-b6d9-7ef11a2f7b70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$8$ per kilowatt-hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499255} {:answer/id #uuid "f26961c7-1ca7-5189-b4cf-cdafb8ec8804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$10$ per kilowatt-hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ce533a25-089b-537e-8dde-61ffcb8f0059" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$$11$  per kilowatt-hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499185} :answer-field/id #uuid "f89aa7c6-1fef-528f-abe7-c21638260283" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499430}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d4a7e4f5-44ad-5538-a987-14a969b63b6d" :question/math-academy-id "q-120267" :question/problem "The price $P,$ in dollars, of electricity in a certain city is directly proportional to the consumption $C,$ measured in kilowatt-hour ($\\text{kWh}).$ When the consumption is $C=24\\,\\text{kWh},$ the price is $P=$264.$ What is the constant of variation?" :question/worked-solution "The price $P$ is directly proportional to the consumption $C,$ so we have\n\n$$\nP=kC,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when the consumption is $C=24\\,\\text{kWh},$ the price is $P=$264.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}264 & =k\\cdot (24) \\\\ 264 & =24k \\\\ \\frac{264}{24} & =k \\\\ 11 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k=\\frac{P}{C},$ we have that the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{dollars}}{\\text{kWh}} & =\\text{dollars per kilowatt-hour}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is $k=$11$ per kilowatt-hour."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138738 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138431 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7df32abc-07bd-5213-a838-50e893ff5759" :knowledge-point/title "Modeling With Direct Variation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d044086-0ee3-503c-9c68-39695bdbd252" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498828}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d044086-0ee3-503c-9c68-39695bdbd252" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498828} :answer-field/id #uuid "458ebd28-0d5b-5e34-ac8f-0865001d94d5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135494}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97912ab9-bc49-5f4d-855e-74d3a88b6f09" :question/math-academy-id "q-210541" :question/problem "The volume of space $V$ a particular gas occupies, measured in liters $(L),$ is directly proportional to its temperature $T,$ measured in Kelvin $(\\text{K}).$ The volume of the gas is $V=48\\,\\text{L}$ when its temperature is $T=360\\,\\text{K}.$ Find the volume of the gas when its temperature is $300\\,\\text{K}.$\n\nThe volume of the gas is\n{{field-1}}$\\text{L}.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "The volume of the gas can be modeled as a direct variation\n\n$$\nV=kT,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that the gas occupies $V=48\\,\\text{L}$ at $T=360\\,\\text{K}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}48 & =k\\cdot 360 \\\\ \\frac{48}{360} & =k \\\\ \\frac{2}{15} & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the equation\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{2}{15}T.\n$$\n\nWe can use this equation to find the volume occupied by the gas when $T=300\\,\\text{K}$:\n\n$$\nV=\\frac{2}{15}\\cdot 300=40\n$$\n\nTherefore, when the temperature of the gas is $300\\,\\text{K},$ its volume is $40\\,\\text{L}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186499522 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138430 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a35a2fee-a1a5-59aa-9390-f73d2898320c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Constant of Variation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0183e978-f0f7-5e61-91f0-df707d2dabe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498732}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0183e978-f0f7-5e61-91f0-df707d2dabe4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186498732} :answer-field/id #uuid "52620407-ea92-57d1-b97d-ba8ffdfea5f7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186499354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab23edc2-70be-5457-b5af-37bf9a82da09" :question/math-academy-id "q-236468" :question/problem "The force $F,$ measured in newtons $(\\text{N})$, needed to extend a spring by some length $l,$ measured in $\\text{m},$ is directly proportional to $l.$ When the extension of the spring is $l=0.005\\,\\text{m},$ the force on the spring is $F=25\\,\\text{N}.$ What is the constant of variation?\n\nThe constant of variation is\n{{field-1}}$\\text{N/m}.$" :question/worked-solution "The force $F$ is directly proportional to the extension $l,$ so we have\n\n$$\nF=kl,\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is the constant of variation.\n\nWe are told that when the extension is $l=0.005\\,\\text{m},$ the force is $F=25\\,\\text{N}.$ We can use this information to solve for $k,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}25 & =k\\cdot (0.005) \\\\ 25 & =0.005k \\\\ \\frac{25}{0.005} & =k \\\\ 5\\,000 & =k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since $k=\\frac{F}{l},$ we have that the units of $k$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{N}}{\\text{m}} & =\\text{N/m}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our final answer is $k=5\\,000\\,\\text{N/m}.$"}]}
